    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> ordered the Great Buddha in 743 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, bad harvests and a rebellion had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> unsettled, and made Tōdai-ji the head of the provincial temples.</li><li>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period it was the central administrative temple for the provincial temples and for six Buddhist schools, and all officially licensed monks were ordained there.</li><li>The Great Buddha Hall has been rebuilt twice after fire; the present hall, finished in 1709, is a third smaller than its predecessor and was given a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> frame between 1974 and 1980.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The 752 eye-opening ceremony for the Great Buddha was attended by 10,000 monks and 4,000 dancers, and the project used much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s bronze and relied entirely on imported gold.</p></aside>
    </section>

