    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">728</span><span class="rf-text">Kinshōsen-ji, the forerunner of Tōdai-ji, was established as a gesture of appeasement for the troubled spirit of Prince Motoi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">743</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> commanded that a very large Buddha image be built, and work began at Shigaraki</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">752</span><span class="rf-text">The eye-opening ceremony celebrating the completion of the Great Buddha was held</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">754</span><span class="rf-text">Ganjin, who had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after six attempts to cross the sea, gave ordination at Tōdai-ji to Empress Kōken, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> and others</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">855</span><span class="rf-text">The head of the Great Buddha fell to the ground, and gifts from across the empire paid for a new head</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1181</span><span class="rf-text">Minamoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a> initiated a reconstruction, entrusted to the monk Chogen</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1709</span><span class="rf-text">The present Great Buddha Hall was finished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text">A second large-scale restoration, begun in 1974 and using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> framing, was completed</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu.jpg" alt="Emperor Shōmu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Shomu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tōdai-ji, whose name means Eastern Great Temple, was an attempt to imitate the Chinese temples of the much-admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. It was founded in 738 and not opened until 752. Its forerunner, Kinshōsen-ji, was established in 728 as a gesture of appeasement for the troubled spirit of Prince Motoi.</p><p>The temple grew out of the instability of the Tenpyō era. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> suffered a series of disasters and epidemics: an alleged coup d'état by Nagaya in 729, a major outbreak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> around 735–737 made worse by several years of poor crops, and a rebellion led by Fujiwara no Hirotsugu in 740. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> had been forced to move the capital four times. In 741 he issued an edict promoting the construction of provincial temples throughout the nation, and in 743 he commissioned the Daibutsu, the Great Buddha. Kinshōsen-ji, still going by that name, was appointed the provincial temple of Yamato Province and the head of all the provincial temples.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was heavily regulated by the state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsuryo">Ritsuryō</a> system, through the Sōgō, the Office of Priestly Affairs.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Tōdai-ji&#8221; cites where it covers Origins, Role in early Japanese Buddhism:</p><ul><li>Ryuichi Abe, The Weaving of Mantra: Kukai and the Construction of Esoteric Buddhist Discourse (Columbia University Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The temple's centrepiece is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> Daibutsu, the Great Buddha of Nara, an image of the Buddha Vairocana (Birushana in Japanese). The temple gives its height as 14.98 metres, with a face of 5.33 metres and eyes of 1.02 metres; the shoulders are 28 metres across, 960 curls cover the head, and the statue weighs 500 tonnes. Its golden halo is 27 metres in diameter and carries 16 images, each 2.4 metres tall. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> have shown a human tooth, pearls, mirrors, swords and jewels inside the knee, believed to be relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a>.</p><p>The statue sits in the Great Buddha Hall, the Daibutsuden, whose roof is carried by 48 lacquered cinnabar pillars. The Nandaimon, the Great South Gate, is guarded by two Nio figures, 8.5 metres tall, the open-mouthed Agyo and the closed-mouthed Ungyo, made by the artists Unkei, Kaikei and their workshop. The original complex also had two pagodas, one on the western and one on the eastern side, each said to be 100 metres tall, along with a library, a lecture hall, a refectory and monks' quarters behind the main hall. The Shōsōin was the storehouse and now holds many artefacts of the Tenpyō period. Tōdai-ji is also the Japanese headquarters of the Kegon school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 743 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> issued a law stating that the people should become directly involved in founding new temples, believing that such piety would inspire the Buddha to protect the country from further disaster. Gyōki and his pupils travelled the provinces asking for donations. According to records kept by Tōdai-ji, more than 2,600,000 people helped to build the Great Buddha and its hall, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wood, metal, cloth or labour, and 350,000 of them worked directly on the statue.</p><p>The statue was built through eight castings over three years, the head and neck being cast together as a separate element. Work began at Shigaraki. After multiple fires and earthquakes, construction resumed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> in 745, when the name Tōdai-ji first appears on record, and the Buddha was completed in 751. The eye-opening ceremony followed in 752, with 10,000 monks and 4,000 dancers attending; the Indian priest Bodhisena performed the eye-opening for Emperor Shōmu. The project cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> greatly: the statue used much of the country's bronze and relied entirely on imported gold. The hall's 48 pillars are 1.5 metres in diameter and 30 metres long, and they carry a blue-tiled roof.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine.jpg" alt="Ise Grand Shrine" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naiku_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N yotarou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to legend, the monk Gyōki went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a> to reconcile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He spent seven days and nights reciting sutras until the oracle declared that Vairocana Buddha was compatible with worship of the sun goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amaterasu">Amaterasu</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period Tōdai-ji served as the central administrative temple for the provincial temples and for the six Buddhist schools then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>: Hossō, Kegon, Jōjitsu, Sanron, Ritsu and Kusha. Letters of the time show that all six had offices there, with administrators, shrines and libraries of their own. Japanese Buddhism still kept the lineage of the Vinaya, the monastic code, and every officially licensed monk had to be ordained under it at Tōdai-ji. In 754 ordination was given by Ganjin, who had reached Japan after twelve years and six attempts to cross the sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, to Empress Kōken, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> were later ordained there too. During Kūkai's administration of the Sōgō, further ceremonies were added, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattva</a> Precepts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> Net Sutra and the esoteric precepts of Kūkai's new Shingon school, and an Abhiseka Hall for initiating monks of the six Nara schools was in place by 829.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo.jpg" alt="Minamoto no Yoritomo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minamoto_no_Yoritomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fujiwara no Takanobu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 855 the head of the Great Buddha suddenly fell to the ground, and gifts from the pious throughout the empire paid for a new head. The statue was damaged again in 1692 and has been recast several times, including after earthquake damage; its present hands date from the Momoyama period (1568–1615) and its head from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1615–1867). The two original pagodas were destroyed by an earthquake. A reconstruction initiated by Minamoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a> in 1181 and entrusted to the monk Chogen standardised existing buildings and strengthened visible pillars.</p><p>The Great Buddha Hall has been rebuilt twice after fire. The present building, finished in 1709, is about 30 per cent smaller than its predecessor, reduced from 11 bays to 7 for lack of funds. By the 19th century its roof and pillars had warped and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a> it risked collapse. A first large-scale restoration, in 1904–1913, could not wholly repair the roof, so support beams were added. The hall escaped the US air raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The second large-scale project began in 1974, mobilised 100,000 people, and was completed in 1980; the roof was removed and fixed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> frames, and the pillars were remodelled with steel and concrete.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The temple's role in maintaining authority declined as the centre of power in Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> sect, and as political power moved from the emperor's capital to the shōgun's base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> after the Genpei war. The Vinaya lineage later died out despite repeated attempts to revive it, so no ordination ceremonies are held at Tōdai-ji now.</p><p>The Great Buddha Hall houses a bronze statue of the Buddha Vairocana that is counted the largest in the world, and until 1998 the hall was counted the world's largest wooden building. Tōdai-ji is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as one of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara, together with seven other sites in the city. The Nio of the Nandaimon were examined and extensively restored by a team of 15 conservators between 1988 and 1993, the first time they had been moved from their niches. The Tōdai-ji Culture Center, with a museum, library and research centre, opened on 10 October 2011. The poet Matsuo Bashō refers to the Great Buddha in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiku">haiku</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris</a> fire of 2019, the temple, which has been rebuilt every time it burned, placed a donation box asking visitors to help rebuild the cathedral.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Tōdai-ji, founded in 738 and opened in 752, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a>'s answer to epidemics and unrest: a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> holding a bronze Vairocana, the Great Buddha, and the centre of Nara-period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and monastic ordination. Fires, earthquakes and decay led to repeated rebuilding, most recently in 1974–1980.</em></p>

